  <h2 id="protein-calories">Protein Calories: The Universal Truth</h2>
  <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-ink"><strong>One gram of protein equals approximately 4 calories.</strong></p>
  <p>This is not an approximation or estimate — it's a scientific fact established through controlled testing using bomb calorimetry. When protein metabolizes, each gram consistently releases 4 kilocalories of energy.</p>

  <h3>Why 4 Calories Per Gram?</h3>
  <p>The 4-kcal measurement comes from the Atwater system, developed by Wilbur Olin Atwater in the late 1800s. He tested foods by burning them in a sealed chamber and measuring the heat released. Protein averages 4 kcal/g because of its nitrogen content and carbon-hydrogen bond structure.</p>

  <h3>Protein vs Other Macros: Calorie Density Comparison</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Macronutrient</th><th scope="col">Calories per gram</th><th scope="col">Thermic effect</th><th scope="col">Satiety index</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Protein</td><td class="text-brand font-semibold">4 kcal/g</td><td class="text-success">20–30% (highest)</td><td class="text-success">High</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Carbohydrates</td><td class="text-brand font-semibold">4 kcal/g</td><td>5–10%</td><td>Moderate</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Fat</td><td class="text-brand font-semibold">9 kcal/g</td><td>0–3%</td><td>Moderate</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Protein and carbs have identical calorie density, but protein provides more metabolic benefits through its thermic effect — your body burns up to 30% of protein calories during digestion.</p>

