  <h3>Complete vs Incomplete Proteins</h3>
  <p>Complete proteins contain all 9 essential amino acids your body cannot make:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Complete (animal):</strong> All meats, fish, eggs, dairy, whey protein</li>
    <li><strong>Complete (plant):</strong> Quinoa, soy products (tofu, tempeh), buckwheat</li>
    <li><strong>Incomplete (plant):</strong> Beans, lentils, nuts, seeds — combine for complete amino acids</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="protein-timing">Protein Timing: Maximizing Muscle Protein Synthesis</h2>
  <p>While total daily protein matters most, timing amplifies results:</p>

  <h3>The 25-40 Gram Rule</h3>
  <p>Research shows 25–40 grams of protein maximally stimulates muscle protein synthesis in adults. Eating more in one meal doesn't help — it's the amount per meal that counts.</p>

  <h3>Optimal Meal Distribution</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Meal</th><th scope="col">Protein</th><th scope="col">Calories from protein</th><th scope="col">Best sources</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Breakfast</td><td>25–30g</td><td>100–120 kcal</td><td>3 eggs + Greek yogurt</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lunch</td><td>30–40g</td><td>120–160 kcal</td><td>150g chicken + quinoa</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Dinner</td><td>30–40g</td><td>120–160 kcal</td><td>150g fish or beef</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Post-workout</td><td>25–30g</td><td>100–120 kcal</td><td>Whey shake within 2 hours</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Protein Before Bed</h3>
  <p>40g casein protein before bed provides amino acids throughout the night, supporting overnight muscle synthesis. That's 160 calories of slow-digesting protein.</p>

  <h2 id="protein-calorie-conversion">Protein to Calories Conversion Guide</h2>
  <p>Master the art of converting between protein grams and calories:</p>

  <h3>Forward Conversion (Grams → Calories)</h3>
  <p>Formula: Protein grams × 4 = Calories</p>
  <ul>
    <li>50g protein × 4 = 200 calories</li>
    <li>100g protein × 4 = 400 calories</li>
    <li>150g protein × 4 = 600 calories</li>
    <li>200g protein × 4 = 800 calories</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Reverse Conversion (Calories → Grams)</h3>
  <p>Formula: Calories ÷ 4 = Protein grams</p>
  <ul>
    <li>500 calories ÷ 4 = 125g protein</li>
    <li>600 calories ÷ 4 = 150g protein</li>
    <li>700 calories ÷ 4 = 175g protein</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="protein-and-calories">Protein and Calories: The Relationship</h2>
  <p>Understanding how protein fits into your overall calorie plan:</p>

  <h3>Protein Percentage of Total Calories</h3>
  <p>Calculate your protein's calorie contribution:</p>
  <p><strong>Formula:</strong> (Protein grams × 4) ÷ Total daily calories × 100 = Protein percentage</p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Total calories</th><th scope="col">Protein grams</th><th scope="col">Protein calories</th><th scope="col">Protein percentage</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1500 kcal</td><td>120g</td><td>480 kcal</td><td>32%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2000 kcal</td><td>150g</td><td>600 kcal</td><td>30%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2500 kcal</td><td>188g</td><td>750 kcal</td><td>30%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>What Percentage Should You Aim For?</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Minimum: 10–15% (sedentary, maintenance focus)</li>
    <li>Optimal: 25–30% (most active people, muscle focus)</li>
    <li>High: 35–40% (fat loss, muscle preservation priority)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="protein-weight-loss">Protein for Weight Loss</h2>
  <p>Higher protein intake is crucial during calorie restriction:</p>

  <h3>Muscle Preservation Strategy</h3>
  <p>During a 500-calorie deficit, aim for 1.6–2.2 g/kg protein. For an 80kg person: 128–176g protein = 512–704 calories from protein. This preserves muscle while dieting.</p>

  <h3>Thermic Effect Advantage</h3>
  <p>Protein burns 20–30% of its calories during digestion. If you eat 600 calories from protein, your body uses 120–180 of those calories just to process it. Carbs: 5–10%. Fat: 0–3%.</p>

  <h2 id="protein-muscle-gain">Protein for Muscle Gain</h2>
  <p>Building muscle requires adequate protein plus a calorie surplus:</p>

  <h3>The Muscle Building Formula</h3>
  <p>1.6–2.2 g/kg protein + 250–500 calorie surplus = optimal muscle growth. Protein provides the building blocks; surplus calories provide the energy.</p>

  <h3>Protein Distribution for Hypertrophy</h3>
  <p>Eat 25–40g protein every 3–4 hours. For 160g daily: 4 meals of 40g each. That's 640 calories from protein across your day.</p>

  <h2 id="protein-calorie-calculator">Protein Calorie Calculator: How to Use It</h2>
  <p>Our <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> does the math for you:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter your weight (kg or lbs)</li>
    <li>Select your activity/goal</li>
    <li>Get grams + calories per day</li>
    <li>Convert to per-meal amounts</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The calculator shows both your protein grams and the calories they provide (grams × 4).</p>

  <h2 id="common-mistakes">Common Protein Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Underestimating needs:</strong> Sedentary RDA (0.8g/kg) is insufficient for active or dieting people</li>
    <li><strong>Poor distribution:</strong> Eating all protein in one meal wastes the 25–40g MPS window</li>
    <li><strong>Ignoring calories:</strong> Not accounting for protein calories in total budget</li>
    <li><strong>Skipping post-workout:</strong> Missing the 2–4 hour muscle-building window</li>
    <li><strong>Relying on supplements:</strong> Whole foods should provide most protein when possible</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="frequently-asked">Frequently Asked Questions About Protein and Calories</h2>
  <div class="not-prose space-y-4">
    <details class="rounded-lg border border-hairline">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">Does cooking protein change the calories?</summary>
      <p class="px-4 pb-3 text-sm text-body">No. Cooking changes weight but not calories. A cooked chicken breast weighs less than raw but has the same protein and calories. The 4 kcal/g rule remains constant.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-lg border border-hairline">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">Are protein calories different from carb calories?</summary>
      <p class="px-4 pb-3 text-sm text-body">Same calories (4 kcal/g) but different effects. Protein increases thermic effect, preserves muscle, and improves satiety more than carbs.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-lg border border-hairline">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">Can I survive on protein calories alone?</summary>
      <p class="px-4 pb-3 text-sm text-body">No. You need fats for hormone production and essential fatty acids. Ketosis requires fat adaptation. A minimum of 15–20% calories from fat is necessary long-term.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-lg border border-hairline">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">What happens if I eat too much protein?</summary>
      <p class="px-4 pb-3 text-sm text-body">Excess protein converts to glucose (gluconeogenesis) or stores as fat if total calories exceed needs. For healthy people, intakes up to 2.5–3 g/kg are safe.</p>
    </details>
  </div>

